 ("F7","Contemporary developments","Modern practice warns of traps the chapter leaves out: an iterator over a list counts positions, so removing items during a for loop skips the item that follows each removed one; replicating a nested list with * shares one inner list; and and and or return the operand that decided the result, not a Boolean, so a length test before an index protects an empty list, while removing many items is better done by assigning a comprehension to a slice of the list.",["P04","P07","P08","P12"]),
